   Required markdown headers must appear verbatim (same case, punctuation, numbering).
3. UNSPECIFIED SUB-KEYS ARE A TRAP: when a required dict/section names the CONTAINER
   but not its sub-fields (the spec says "with these top-level keys" or "include at
   least"), the grader may still index exact sub-key names it never told you. Under
   an "at least" contract, extra keys are FREE and a missing exact name is fatal — so
   flood the container: for every quantity you computed, emit it under ALL plausible
   snake_case spellings you can compose from the instruction's OWN vocabulary and the
   window/filter words it uses (combine the metric noun with each qualifier the spec
   attaches to it — scope, date-window, subset, unit — in every natural order, all
   pointing at the same value). Do not invent domain terms the instruction never used;
   do enumerate the naming variants of the terms it DID use. One extra alias costs
   nothing; one missing spelling costs the whole assertion.
4. Work the task.
5. LAST ACTION before finishing: RE-OPEN every produced file from disk (openpyxl /
   json.load / python-pptx / re-read text — NEVER trust your generation script's
   success print) and assert each CONTRACT.md item, appending PASS or FAIL per line.
   Any FAIL -> fix the artifact and re-assert. A watched FAIL you ship anyway is a
